<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<handout xml:id="day-5-notes">
  <title>Day 5: Limits and Asymptotes (Section 2.6b) and the Sandwich Theorem</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      <term>Last Time:</term> We learned about one-sided and infinite limits, and how to determine when limits exist.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>Today:</term> We want to find the relationships between limits and asymptotes, and learn how to deal with a few last strange cases.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-vertical-asymptotes">
      <title>Vertical Asymptotes</title>
      <p>
        We saw last time (Day 4) that when we have a vertical asymptote, we also get an <fillin characters="14"/> limit, which takes the form <m>\left(\dfrac{\text{nonzero \#}}{0}\right)</m> after direct substitution. This relationship is even closer than that<mdash/>we can use limits to prove the existence of vertical asymptotes.
          </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="def-vertical-asymptote">
      <title>Definition</title>
      <p>
        A line <m>x=a</m> is a <fillin characters="15"/> asymptote of the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> if either
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 8% 44%" valign="middle">
        <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^+} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="8"/></p>
        <p>or</p>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to a^-} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="8"/></p>
      </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-vertical-asymptotes">
      <title>Problem 1</title>
      <p>
        Find all vertical asymptotes of the function <m>f(x)=\dfrac{x+1}{x^2-1}</m>. Be sure to justify your answers using limits.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li workspace="0.8in">
          <p><term>Step 1:</term> Note that an infinite limit could occur when <fillin characters="30"/>.</p>
        </li>
        <li workspace="1.8in">
          <p><term>Step 2:</term> We can now use limits to verify vertical asymptotes.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        So we see that finding places where the <fillin characters="24"/> of a rational function is <m>0</m> gives us a good start on <m>x</m> values we should check for asymptotes, but we really need to use <fillin characters="15"/> to prove that we actually have one.
      </p>
</paragraphs>

</page>

  <page>

<paragraphs xml:id="infinite-limits">
      <title>Infinite Limits</title>

      <p>
        Let's shift gears a little here: we have a decent understanding now of how to evaluate limits that end up being <em>equal to</em> <m>\pm \infty</m><mdash/>but what about limits <em>at</em> infinity? That is, how do we think about things of the form
        <me>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) \qquad \text{or} \qquad \displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)?</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The <q>trick</q> here is that just like vertical asymptotes capture what's going on when our function gets arbitrarily large (positive or negative) in the <m>y</m> direction, <fillin characters="28"/> asymptotes are really giving us information about what happens to <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> becomes arbitrarily large (positive or negative).
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  
    <paragraphs xml:id="def-horizontal-asymptote">
      <title>Definition</title>
      <p>
        A line <m>y=b</m> is a <fillin characters="18"/> asymptote of the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> if either
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="44% 8% 44%" valign="middle">
        <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p>
        <p>or</p>
        <p><m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/></p>
      </sidebyside>

       <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            To find all possible horizontal asymptotes of <m>f(x)</m>, we compute both <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)</m> and <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)</m>. 
        </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            A function has <fillin characters="20"/> <fillin characters="10"/> distinct horizontal asymptotes.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      
      <p>
        <term>Example:</term>
      </p>
      <sidebyside width="45%">
        <image source="pictures/sec26bfa24pic1.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Graph illustrating an exponential function approaching a horizontal asymptote as x approaches positive or negative infinity.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
      <p>
        Again, this idea is hopefully intuitive based on a graph, but how do we discover what's going on by looking at a function algebraically? Here are 2 sets of limits that help us find horizontal asymptotes:
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-rational-horizontal-asymptotes">
      <title>Problem 2</title>
      <p>
        Find all horizontal asymptotes of <m>g(x)=\dfrac{x^2-2x+4}{3x^2-5}</m> by computing the following limits:
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="55% 40%" valign="top">
        <stack>
          <p workspace="2in">
            <strong>(a)</strong> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} \dfrac{x^2-2x+4}{3x^2-5}</m>
          </p>
        </stack>
        <stack>
          <p workspace="1.2in">
            <strong>(b)</strong> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} \dfrac{x^2-2x+4}{3x^2-5}</m> <em>(exercise!)</em>
          </p>
          <image source="pictures/sec26fa24pic8.png">
            <shortdescription>
              Graph of y = 1/x showing both branches approaching y = 0 as x approaches positive and negative infinity.
            </shortdescription>
          </image>
          <p>
            A hint for this problem: Recall the graph of <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m>.
          </p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="p-horizontal-asymptote-strategy">
      <title>Strategy</title>
      <p>
        Let's write down generally the strategy that we used above:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <term>Strategy:</term> If direct substitution yields an indeterminate form of <m>\left(\pm\dfrac{\infty}{\infty}\right)</m>, divide <fillin characters="10"/> term in both the numerator and denominator by the <fillin characters="12"/> term (in absolute value) in the entire function.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em><term>Alternative:</term> Dividing by the <fillin characters="10"/> term (in absolute value) in the <fillin characters="20"/> also works<mdash/>it simply gives a different-looking (but equivalent)
                        expression.
                        </em>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-exponential-horizontal-asymptotes">
      <title>Problem 3</title>
      <p>
        Find all horizontal asymptotes of <m>f(x)=\dfrac{2^{3x} - 2^x + 5}{2^{2x}+7}</m>. <em>Recall that:</em> <m>\dfrac{2^a}{2^b} = 2^{a-b} = \dfrac{1}{2^{b-a}}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Recall that:</em> <m>\dfrac{2^a}{2^b} = 2^{a-b} = \dfrac{1}{2^{b-a}}</m>.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="55% 40%" valign="top">
        <stack>
          <p workspace="2in">
            <strong>(a)</strong> <em>(Exercise)</em> Consider <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)</m><br/>
          </p>
          <p><em>Hint: Let's use the alternative approach.</em></p>
        </stack>
        <stack>
          <p workspace="2in">
            <strong>(b)</strong> Consider <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} f(x)</m>
          </p>
          <p><em>Hint: How the exponential function <m>2^x</m> behaves as <m>x</m> approaches negative infinity.</em></p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

       </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-vertical-horizontal-asymptotes">
      <title>Recap</title>

      <p>
        Whenever we find asymptotes, we look for infinite limits (<fillin characters="15"/> asymptotes) or limits at infinity (<fillin characters="20"/> asymptotes).
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="checkpoint-oscillating-asymptote">
      <title>Problem 4</title>
      <p>
        Let's consider the following problem to get thinking about limits again. Find all horizontal asymptotes of the function <m>f(x)=\dfrac{\cos(x)}{e^x}</m>.
      </p>

      <sidebyside widths="46% 48%" valign="top">
        <stack>
          <p>Recall that:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\cos(x)</m> oscillates between <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5"/> and <m>y =</m> <fillin characters="5"/> with a period of <fillin characters="5"/>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\cos(0) =</m> <fillin characters="4"/>, <m>\cos(\pi/2) =</m> <fillin characters="4"/>, <m>\cos(\pi) = -</m><fillin characters="4"/>, etc.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </stack>
        <image source="pictures/sec26fa24pic7.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Graph of y = cos(x) oscillating periodically between y = -1 and y = 1 with period 2 pi.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>

      <p workspace="1.2in">
        First, look at <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to -\infty} \dfrac{\cos(x)}{e^x}</m> (a graph might help<mdash/>you may use Desmos):
      </p>
      <p>
        So there is <fillin characters="6"/> horizontal asymptote in the negative <m>x</m>-direction.
      </p>
      <p>
        This shows another way a limit fails to exist: If our function oscillates <fillin characters="15"/> getting infinitely close to a single number, the limit <fillin characters="10"/>.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>

  <page>
    <paragraphs xml:id="p-oscillating-phenomena">
      <title>Two Interesting Phenomena of Limits</title>

      <sidebyside widths="46% 46%" margins="2% 2%" valign="top">
        <stack>
          <image source="pictures/sec26fa24pic6.png">
            <shortdescription>
              Graph of a function oscillating with unbounded amplitude so it never approaches a single number.
            </shortdescription>
          </image>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Oscillating without getting infinitely close to a single number</p></li>
            <li><p>The limit DNE.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </stack>
        <stack>
          <image source="pictures/sec26fa24pic3.png">
            <shortdescription>
              Graph of a damped oscillating function whose amplitude shrinks toward one as x approaches negative infinity.
            </shortdescription>
          </image>
          <ul>
            <li><p>Oscillating and <fillin characters="20"/> infinitely close to a single number</p></li>
            <li><p>The limit <fillin characters="8"/>.</p></li>
          </ul>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p>
        Now to make sure we have all horizontal asymptotes, we also check <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} f(x)</m>. Based on the graph, this limit appears to be <fillin characters="6"/>, but we need a careful algebraic method to confirm it.
      </p>
      <p>
         Here's an effective way to handle limits with bounded oscillations:
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="thm-sandwich-theorem">
      <title>The Sandwich (or Squeeze) Theorem</title>

      <sidebyside widths="38% 58%" valign="top">
        <image source="pictures/sec26fa24pic4.png">
          <shortdescription>
            Diagram illustrating the Sandwich Theorem with f(x) trapped between upper function h(x) and lower function g(x), all meeting at L.
          </shortdescription>
        </image>
        <stack>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>g(x) \leq</m> <fillin characters="6"/> <m>\leq h(x)</m> on some interval containing <m>c</m> (except possibly at <m>c</m> itself). If
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to c} g(x)</m> <fillin characters="4"/> <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to c} h(x)</m> <fillin characters="4"/> <m>L</m>,
          </p>
          <p>
            then we must have <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to c} f(x) =</m> <fillin characters="6"/> also.
          </p>
          <p>
            <term>Note:</term> In the theorem, <m>c</m> can be finite or infinite.
          </p>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>

      <p workspace="2in">
        This theorem will help us finish our problem. We'll look again at <m>\displaystyle \lim_{x \to \infty} \dfrac{\cos(x)}{e^x}</m>:
      </p>
    </paragraphs>
  </page>
</handout>